# Target C-II — balanced antipodal strip reaction can only occur with one switch orientation

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only exact structural strengthening  
**Scope:** the balanced-antipodal branch of the adjacent-strip sparse-force theorem  
**Dependencies:** adjacent-strip switching one-arc order; sparse KKT layer reaction; convex-hull extremality of actual diameter endpoints  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The adjacent-strip sparse-force theorem leaves one exceptional reaction branch:

\[
G:=Aa+Bb=0,
\]

which is equivalent to

\[
\boxed{b=-a,\qquad A=B.}
\tag{BA.1}
\]

In that branch the exact switching reaction is

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_h n_h=0,
\qquad \beta_h>0,
}
\tag{BA.2}
\]

where every inward switching vector has the form

\[
n_h=m_h-x_h,
\qquad |n_h|=D,
\tag{BA.3}
\]

with `m_h` the endpoint in the middle layer and `x_h` the endpoint outside it.

The ordered switching-interface theorem shows that (BA.2) is incompatible with having both left-switch and right-switch diameter edges.

### Theorem BA

In the canonical adjacent strip, if the exact switching reaction vanishes, then all switching diameter edges have the same interval orientation:

\[
\boxed{
H_{sw}=H_L\quad\text{or}\quad H_{sw}=H_R.
}
\tag{BA.4}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\text{balanced antipodality }(b=-a,A=B)
\Longrightarrow
\text{the switching interface is one-sided.}
}
\tag{BA.5}
\]

Thus the only branch in which the sparse force reaction can require a three-bar positive zero-sum certificate is already forced into a single start/stop orientation of the laminar diameter intervals.

---

## 1. Mixed switching orientations put all middle endpoints on one hull arc

Assume for contradiction that both a left-switch edge and a right-switch edge occur.

Use the exact four-block outer-boundary order from the audited adjacent switching theorem:

\[
B_L,\ B_M^+,\ B_R,\ B_M^-.
\tag{BA.6}
\]

That theorem proves that, once both switch orientations occur, every switching middle endpoint lies on one common middle boundary arc

\[
B_M^\star\in\{B_M^+,B_M^-\}.
\tag{BA.7}
\]

Every exterior endpoint of a switching edge lies in

\[
B_L\cup B_R,
\tag{BA.8}
\]

which is contained in the complementary outer-boundary arc between the two endpoints of `B_M^star`.

Actual diameter endpoints are extreme points of the source convex hull.  In particular none of the participating endpoints can lie in the relative interior of a hull side.

---

## 2. Complementary open hull arcs are strictly line-separated

Let `p,q` be the two boundary points delimiting the open arc `B_M^star`.

If `p,q` lie on distinct supporting faces, the line `pq` meets the convex hull in the chord `[p,q]`.  The two open boundary arcs from `p` to `q` lie in the two opposite open half-planes bounded by that line.  Hence there is an affine functional `ell` and a choice of sign such that

\[
\ell(m)>0
\quad(m\in B_M^\star\text{ an extreme source vertex}),
\tag{BA.9}
\]

while

\[
\ell(x)<0
\quad(x\text{ an extreme source vertex on the complementary open arc}).
\tag{BA.10}
\]

The only degenerate possibility is that `p,q` lie on one common hull side.  Then one of the two boundary arcs between them is contained in that side and has no extreme source vertex in its relative interior.  Because under the mixed-switch assumption both a middle switching endpoint and an exterior switching endpoint exist, neither participating endpoint class can be confined to such an empty open face segment.  Thus the participating middle and exterior endpoint sets are again strictly separated by a line (equivalently, perturb the common supporting line infinitesimally toward the nonempty complementary arc).

Consequently there is a single affine functional `ell` satisfying (BA.9)--(BA.10) for **every** switching edge.

---

## 3. Zero switching reaction would equate two separated barycenters

Write the zero reaction (BA.2) using (BA.3):

\[
0
=\sum_h\beta_h(m_h-x_h)
=\sum_h\beta_hm_h-\sum_h\beta_hx_h.
\tag{BA.11}
\]

Let

\[
B_{sw}:=\sum_h\beta_h>0.
\]

Then (BA.11) says that the two positive weighted barycenters agree:

\[
\boxed{
{1\over B_{sw}}\sum_h\beta_hm_h
=
{1\over B_{sw}}\sum_h\beta_hx_h.
}
\tag{BA.12}
\]

But applying the separating functional gives

\[
{1\over B_{sw}}\sum_h\beta_h\ell(m_h)>0,
\tag{BA.13}
\]

and

\[
{1\over B_{sw}}\sum_h\beta_h\ell(x_h)<0,
\tag{BA.14}
\]

contradicting (BA.12).

Therefore mixed left/right switching orientations are impossible when the reaction is zero.  This proves (BA.4).

---

## 4. Updated sparse-reaction frontier

The adjacent-strip reaction now has the exact split

```text
G != 0:
    exact KKT reaction generated by one or two switching diameter bars;

G = 0:
    b = -a and A = B;
    every switching bar has the same start/stop orientation;
    positive zero-sum force balance uses the one-sided switching family only.
```

The generic branch is already a one/two-bar force problem.  The exceptional branch is no longer an arbitrary three-bar balance inside the full prefix/hub/suffix caterpillar: it is a **one-sided balanced-antipodal interface**.

A next closure theorem can therefore attack the one-sided branch using the single-cut cyclic order and Section-157 resultant geometry, without carrying the mixed left/right transition hub.

No exact triangle or strict-diameter competitor is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact reduction of the balanced-antipodal zero-reaction branch to one-sided switching. C-II and Level I remain open.
